      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the incidence of back pain in children admitted with sickle cell disease so as to increase awareness of this disease in black children presenting with back pain or discomfort."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A retrospective review."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "The Children's Hospital of Eastern Ontario in Ottawa, a tertiary care, university affiliated centre."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Thirty children were treated for active sickle cell disease between 1990 and 1996. Eleven (5 boys, 6 girls) suffered vascular-occlusive phenomena occurring in bone, referred to as \"bony crises,\" requiring a total of 49 admissions."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Clinical manifestations of spinal involvement by sickle cell disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "The spine represented the second most common area of bone involvement (26%) exceeded only by the knee (35%). The vertebral level affected was lumbosacral in 66% of cases, followed by thoracic in 22% and cervical in only 12%. Eighty-six percent of the children with spinal pain were anemic upon presentation, 71% had an elevated leukocyte count, 15% were hyponatremic and 15% were hyperkalemic. Minimal physical signs in the spine were noted, other than a local tenderness over the spinous process in 71% and a decreased range of back motion in 17%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Sickle cell disease is becoming more common in Canada as a result of increasing immigration from African countries and should always be considered as a possible cause of back pain in a black child."
        }
      ]},
